* Example for 1 sand fraction (uniform sand); wavedata from file; ROUGHNESS COMPUTED AUTOMATICALLY
* INPUTFILE; READ DIAGNOSE FILE IF FILE DOES NOT RUN
* Text lines have * at first position; data have no *
*                               1
*             \                 1       /
*              \                1      /
*               \               1     /
*                > + wave angle 1    < - wave angle
*                               1
*    -------->+ Current         1   - current <------------
* _____________________________________________________________
*                            COAST
*
* Definition of directions is based on observer at beach with face to the sea (=shore normal)
* Incidence wave angles are defined with respect to shore normal
* Waves coming from left of shore normal have a positive angle to shore normal
* Longshore current coming from left of shore normal has a positive value
* Waves coming from right of shore normal have a negative angle to shore normal
* Longshore current coming from right of shore normal has a negative value
* X-axis starts offshore;x=0 at offshore boundary; positive in landward direction
*
* Options output hydrodynamics 0/1/2
* 0= no output 1=output hydrodynamics weighted over all wave classes,
* 2=output hydrodynamics per wave class
  2
* table length of tidal schematization (number of values as function of time);
    2
* specify Vlong and Ws as function of time; values are interpolated linearly at
* each time step (if end time is smaller than computation time, the cycle is rep
* VNUL1= longshore velocity at x=0 (positive and negative, see above sketch)
* VNUL2= cross-shore velocity at x=0 as function of time (pos. in landward dir.)
*
* Ws=tidal water level to MSL (pos. upward, neg. downward)
*   T (sec)    Vnul1(m/s)  Vnul2(m/s)  Ws(m)
    0.           0.5        .05           0.
   72000.        0.5        .05           0.
*  table length of bottom schematization
    3
*   X=cross=shore coordinate, HMSL=depth to MSL (neg below MSL), NSTAP=nmber of
*   X(m)  HMSL(m) NSTAP
     0.    -10.     100
     500   -3.     50
     700.   -2.
* minimum water depth for calculation hgrens
*1/0 beach  no beach
  0.25   0
* table length storm (wind-induced) set-up HSTORM
    2
* HSTORM is constant along profile and specified as a function of time;
* interpolation at each time step and cyclic repetition if end time is smaller
* than total computation time
*   T(s)    HSTORM(m)
     0.        0.
     72000.    0.
* option=1/2/-2
*              1= specify wave classes for 1 condition;
*              2= compute wave classes (Rayleigh) based on Hrms for NHT conditio
*             -2= same as 2 but NHT, Time, Hrms, T, Teta, w10 from file
 -2
*  In case of option 1, specify Number of wave classes, H= wave height at x=0,
*  T=wave period, P=percentage of occurrence, TETA= wave angle to coast normal
* Number of wave classes (option1, put * in front of lines if not required)
* 9
*    H     T    P    TETA   W10
*                               H = wave height(m)
*                               T = wave period(s)
*                               P = perc entage of occurrence
*                               TETA = angle wave incidence w.r.t. coast normal
*                                      (positive or negative)
*                               W10= wind velocity (m/s) at 10 m above surface
*   0.3    2      0.14      0.  0.
